  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, the alert rule is enabled, and the response-code panel is renamed.\n\n**1. Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, ID `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`)\n- Time field: `timestamp`.\n- Main fields: `@timestamp`, `timestamp`, `response` (string, with `response.keyword`), `request`, `url`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `agent`, `host`, `machine.*`, `tags`, and some others.\n\n**2. Dashboard 'S4-1a681a8a Web logs'** (ID `b1570478-a161-4833-bf03-08fb2182623f`)\n- Panel 1: \"Requests over time\", an XY line chart using ES|QL count per time bucket.\n- Panel 2: \"Requests by response code\", a donut chart with count split by `response.keyword`, top 10 terms.\n\n**3. Alert rule 'S4-1a681a8a 5xx errors'** (ID `525f2fe6-14df-40ce-a391-1ebfcd903083`)\n- Type: `.es-query`, DSL search on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Condition: `response.keyword` matches regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`, so it covers 500\u2013599 exactly.\n- Fires when count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window, checked every 1 minute.\n- Created enabled, so no separate enable call was needed.\n\n**4. Panel edit**\n- Renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\" with a PUT on the dashboard. The re-fetched dashboard shows both panels and the new title.\n- Read the rule back: `enabled: True`, status `ok`, threshold `[10] >`, window `5m`.\n\nI didn't run the 5xx query on its own to check the match count; the rule itself executed successfully.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- The panels are inline dashboard panels, not saved Visualizations.\n- The response-code donut shows the top 10 codes, not the API example's top 5.\n- The rule has no actions, so it records alerts but doesn't notify anyone.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
